// RED TEAM round 3, lens: type-confusion.
//
// kryos-mir/src/lower.rs::resolve_struct_literal_name / extract_param_binding
// (read directly this session) has a documented-in-comments gap: a generic
// struct field typed `map<K, Option<V>>` cannot have its `V` binding
// recovered by field-based inference, because `extract_param_binding`'s
// `TypeExpr::Generic` arm only special-cases `name == "map"` and recurses
// into the map's key/value TypeExprs -- but when the recursive call lands on
// `Option<V>` (also a `TypeExpr::Generic`, name == "Option"), the same arm's
// `if name == "map"` guard fails and the function returns `None` with NO
// further recursion into `Option`'s own args. So the V binding for a struct
// field of shape `map<str, Option<V>>` is NEVER found by field inference.
//
// The comment at lower.rs:15138-15162 says there's a FALLBACK for exactly
// this case ("ret_hint"): if the struct literal sits inside a generic
// function whose OWN declared return type is `ThisStruct<V>`, the already-
// resolved return-type instantiation is used instead. But that fallback only
// fires when `ctx.current_ret_ty` is literally `Struct("Registry___<X>")` at
// lowering time -- i.e. the enclosing function's DECLARED return type. If
// the struct literal is built directly in a NON-generic context (e.g.
// `main`, return type `()`), there is no ret_hint, and `extract_param_binding`
// returns `None`, so the code falls all the way to `.unwrap_or(MirType::I64)`
// -- V is silently bound to i64 regardless of the field's real element type.
//
// Attack: construct `Registry<V>` generic-struct literals directly in `main`
// (unit return, no ret_hint) at THREE different real V types (i64, f64, str)
// in the SAME run. If the hypothesis is right, every instantiation's V binds
// to the same fallback (i64), so the struct's tracked field metadata says
// `entries: map<str, Option<i64>>` for all three -- and reading back the f64
// / str instances through the match arm renders the raw i64/f64 bit pattern
// or a garbage pointer-as-string instead of the real value. Assert via
// `to_string`/prints, not bit-level checks, per this round's instructions.
//
// RULED OUT, live, both backends, HEAD `5cccaf4` (compiler/target/release/
// kryos.exe, read-only, no rebuild). `kryos run`: i64_val=42, f64_val=3.5,
// str_val=hello-world, rc=0. `kryos build --release` + running the produced
// binary: identical output, rc=0. The `extract_param_binding` gap for
// `map<K, Option<V>>` is real (confirmed by reading `resolve_struct_literal_
// name`/`extract_param_binding` in kryos-mir/src/lower.rs -- there is no
// `TypeExpr::Optional`/`Generic{name:"Option",..}` recursion arm, so field-
// based V inference does structurally fail for this shape), but it does not
// manifest as an observable wrong value for a `let x: Registry<V> = ..`
// literal -- the explicit LET type annotation apparently supplies the
// correct V through a separate, independent path that wins before the
// gapped field-inference fallback matters. See the `_v2` sibling file for
// the same probe with the annotation removed (call-argument construction,
// no `let` hint) -- also ruled out, same evidence, same HEAD.

use std::option::{Option, Some, None}

struct Registry<V> {
    entries: map<str, Option<V>>
}

@capabilities(all)
fn main() {
    let ri: Registry<i64> = Registry { entries: {"k": Some(42)} }
    let rf: Registry<f64> = Registry { entries: {"k": Some(3.5)} }
    let rs: Registry<str> = Registry { entries: {"k": Some("hello-world")} }

    match ri.entries["k"] {
        Some(x) => println("i64_val=" + to_string(x)),
        None() => println("i64_val=NONE"),
    }
    match rf.entries["k"] {
        Some(x) => println("f64_val=" + to_string(x)),
        None() => println("f64_val=NONE"),
    }
    match rs.entries["k"] {
        Some(x) => println("str_val=" + x),
        None() => println("str_val=NONE"),
    }
}
